# Hospital Appointment & Slot Booking System

A hospital appointment manager written in pure Java, built for
**CSE2006 – Programming in Java** (VITyarthi *Build Your Own Project*).
It ships with **two front ends over one shared backend**: the original
console UI, and a browser UI served by the JDK's own `com.sun.net.httpserver`
— no framework, no external jar, no application server.

The system lets patients find a doctor, see a live slot grid and book a
consultation; lets doctors run their day and issue prescriptions; and gives
the administrator oversight and analytics over the whole appointment book.
Two background threads handle reminders and stale-slot cleanup.

```
        ┌────────────────┐        ┌────────────────┐
        │  Console UI    │        │  Browser UI     │
        │  com.hospital.ui│       │  com.hospital.web│
        └───────┬────────┘        └────────┬────────┘
                └──────────────┬───────────┘
                     ServiceRegistry (shared)
                AuthService · SlotService · BookingService
                PrescriptionService · ReportService
                     Repository<T> (CSV or JDBC)
```

Both entry points build the exact same `ServiceRegistry`. Booking rules,
the per-doctor lock, validation, persistence and the two background threads
are identical either way — only how a person reaches them differs.

---

## Overview

Walk-in hospital desks lose time to a manual register: the same slot gets
promised twice, cancellations are never reflected, and nobody can answer
"how busy was Dr. Mehta last week?" without counting by hand. This project
replaces that register with a role-based system where a slot can be held by
exactly one patient at a time — a guarantee that is enforced with thread
synchronization and proved by an automated 25-thread race test.

**Three major functional modules**

| # | Module | What it does |
|---|--------|--------------|
| 1 | **User Management** | Self-registration, salted-hash login, three roles, account enable/disable, password change |
| 2 | **Slot & Booking Engine** | Slot-grid generation from each doctor's working window, conflict-free booking, cancellation with late-cancel billing, rescheduling with rollback |
| 3 | **Records & Analytics** | Prescriptions against completed consultations, revenue/load/demand dashboards, CSV export, file-based audit log |

---

## Features

**Patient**
- Browse doctors, filter by specialization
- View the full slot grid for any date (FREE / BOOKED / ELAPSED)
- Book a slot, with the next free date suggested when a day is full
- Cancel (free up to 2 hours before, 50% charged after) and reschedule
- View appointment history and prescriptions

**Doctor**
- Today's schedule or any chosen date, with occupancy percentage
- Mark a consultation completed
- Issue a prescription (diagnosis, medicine list, advice)
- Cancel an appointment on the patient's behalf
- Earnings summary, split by realised and gross billable

**Administrator**
- Onboard doctors with specialization, fee, room, working hours, slot length
- List and enable/disable any account
- View every appointment for a date
- Analytics dashboard: status breakdown, revenue by doctor, demand by
  specialization, daily load histogram, top doctors, cancellation rate
- Export the appointment book to `reports/appointments_<date>.csv`
- Tail the system log

**Running in the background**
- `reminder-daemon` — extends `Thread`, wakes every 60s, logs reminders for
  appointments starting within 24 hours
- `auto-expiry` — implements `Runnable`, closes slots that elapsed while
  still `BOOKED` so reports stay honest

---

## Technologies & tools used

| Area | Choice |
|---|---|
| Language | Java 17+ (developed and tested on OpenJDK 21) |
| External libraries | **None** — standard library only |
| Persistence | Pipe-delimited files under `data/`, plus an optional JDBC backend |
| Concurrency | `Thread`, `Runnable`, `synchronized`, `volatile`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, `AtomicInteger`, `CountDownLatch` |
| Security | Salted SHA-256 via `java.security.MessageDigest` |
| Testing | A hand-written assertion harness (`MiniTest`) — 64 assertions, no JUnit jar required |
| Version control | Git |

### Syllabus coverage

| Unit | Where it appears |
|---|---|
| 1 — Flow control, I/O | Menu loops, `switch`, validation branches, console `Reader` |
| 2 — OOP | `User` → `Patient`/`Doctor`/`Admin`; abstract `CsvRepository` and `RoleMenu` (template method); `Billable` interface with a default method; enums with constructors; `AppLogger` singleton; `instanceof` mapping; reflection in `Class.forName` driver loading |
| 3 — Exceptions & threads | `HospitalException` hierarchy with error codes and chaining; `ReminderDaemon` (extends Thread) and `AutoExpiryWorker` (implements Runnable); per-doctor `synchronized` booking |
| 4 — Collections & streams | `List`, `Map`, `TreeMap`, `EnumMap`, `Comparator` sorting; `BufferedReader`/`BufferedWriter`/`PrintWriter` for data files, logs and reports |
| 5 — JDBC | `DatabaseManager` (external `db.properties`, `DriverManager`, DDL) and `JdbcAppointmentRepository` (`PreparedStatement`, `ResultSet`, try-with-resources) implementing the same `Repository<Appointment>` contract |

