## Project structure

```
HospitalSlotBooking/
├── src/com/hospital/
│   ├── Main.java                  console entry point, shutdown hook
│   ├── model/                     User, Patient, Doctor, Admin, Appointment,
│   │                              Slot, Prescription, Billable, 3 enums
│   ├── dao/                       Repository<T>, CsvRepository<T>, User/Appointment/
│   │                              Prescription repositories, DatabaseManager,
│   │                              JdbcAppointmentRepository
│   ├── service/                   AuthService, SlotService, BookingService,
│   │                              PrescriptionService, ReportService,
│   │                              ServiceRegistry, DataSeeder
│   ├── concurrent/                ReminderDaemon, AutoExpiryWorker
│   ├── exception/                 HospitalException + 5 specific types
│   ├── ui/                        ConsoleApp, RoleMenu, Admin/Doctor/PatientMenu
│   ├── web/                       WebMain, WebServer, SessionManager, Html, WebUtil
│   │                              (browser front end — built on the JDK's own
│   │                               com.sun.net.httpserver, zero extra jars)
│   └── util/                      AppLogger, PasswordUtil, IdGenerator,
│                                  Validator, CsvUtil, ConsoleInput, TableFormatter
├── test/com/hospital/test/        MiniTest, TestRunner (64 assertions)
├── docs/                          ARCHITECTURE.md, REPORT_GUIDE.md
├── config/db.properties.example   optional JDBC settings
├── build.sh · run.sh · run_web.sh · run_tests.sh · run.bat · run_web.bat
├── README.md · statement.md
└── data/ · logs/ · reports/       created at runtime
```

50 source files across 8 packages.

### How the web layer is built

- `WebServer` routes every request to the same `ServiceRegistry` the console
  uses — one method per screen (e.g. `patientSlots`, `doBook`, `adminAnalytics`).
- `Html` renders pages with plain `String` building (no template engine),
  escaping every user-supplied value before it goes into the page.
- `SessionManager` keeps a `ConcurrentHashMap<token, User>` and a login cookie
  — no external session library needed.
- Every page is server-rendered HTML with plain HTML forms; a POST is
  followed by a redirect (the same pattern any Servlet-based app uses), so
  refreshing a page never re-submits a booking.
- Business-rule failures (a `HospitalException`) are caught centrally in the
  router and turned into a flash message on the page the user came from —
  the browser never sees a raw stack trace.

---

## Install & run

**Requirement:** JDK 17 or newer (`java -version` to check). Nothing else —
the web front end below needs no extra dependency either; `HttpServer` is
part of the standard JDK.

### Option A — Browser UI (recommended for a demo)

```bash
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd HospitalSlotBooking
./run_web.sh          # starts on http://localhost:8080
```

Windows: `run_web.bat`. Pick a different port with `./run_web.sh 9090`.
Open the printed URL in any browser. Stop with `Ctrl+C`.

### Option B — Console UI

```bash
./run.sh
```

Windows: `run.bat`. Add `--verbose` to mirror log lines to the console:
`./run.sh --verbose`.

### Manual build (either front end)

```bash
mkdir -p out
find src test -name "*.java" > .sources
javac -d out @.sources
java -cp out com.hospital.Main          # console
java -cp out com.hospital.web.WebMain   # browser, port 8080
```

Both front ends read and write the same `data/` folder, so you can switch
between them freely — a booking made in the browser shows up in the console
app and vice versa.

### Demo accounts (created automatically on first run)

| Role | Username | Password |
|---|---|---|
| Admin | `admin` | `admin123` |
| Doctor | `dr.mehta` (Cardiology) | `doctor123` |
| Doctor | `dr.rao` (Orthopaedics) | `doctor123` |
| Doctor | `dr.khan` (Paediatrics) | `doctor123` |
| Doctor | `dr.nair` (General) | `doctor123` |
| Patient | `arjun` / `priya` / `kabir` | `patient123` |

Delete the `data/` folder to reset to a clean state.

### Optional: switch on the JDBC backend

```bash
cp config/db.properties.example config/db.properties
# edit: set db.enabled=true, fill in driver, url, user, password
# place the driver jar in lib/
java -cp "out:lib/*" com.hospital.Main
```

With no `db.properties` the application logs a line and stays on file
storage, so it always runs on a bare JDK.

---

## Testing

```bash
./run_tests.sh
```

The suite runs inside `build/testrun`, so your real `data/` folder is never
touched. Expected output ends with:

```
  RESULT: 64 passed, 0 failed
```

What it covers:

| Suite | Assertions |
|---|---|
| Input validation | Names, phones, ages, passwords, past/far-future dates |
| Password hashing | Plain text absent, verify/reject, per-hash salting, malformed input |
| CSV encoding | Pipes inside values escape and round-trip |
| Slot generation | Slot counts, boundaries, partial trailing slots dropped |
| Booking happy path | Status, fee copy, end-time arithmetic, id format, slot consumed |
| Booking rejections | Double booking, past slot, off-grid time, outside hours, patient clash |
| **Concurrency** | **25 threads race one slot → exactly 1 wins, 24 rejected, 1 row stored** |
| Cancellation & billing | Early vs late cancel, slot release, no-show 50%, tax, double-cancel, wrong user |
| Consultation flow | Prescribe-before-complete blocked, duplicate blocked, list immutability |
| Reporting | Status coverage, revenue sort order, rate bounds, export file contents |
| Background workers | Thread state NEW → alive → terminated, daemon flag, cycle count |

The concurrency test is the one worth watching: remove the `synchronized`
block in `BookingService.book()` and it starts failing intermittently.

---

## Design notes

- **Service layer talks only to `Repository<T>`.** That is why the JDBC
  implementation can replace the CSV one without a single service change.
- **Locks are per doctor, not global.** Two patients booking different
  doctors never block each other; two patients booking the same doctor are
  serialised. The lock objects live in a `ConcurrentHashMap`.
- **Every check that could reject a booking happens inside the lock.**
  Validating first and writing later would leave a gap another thread could
  use.
- **Reschedule rolls back.** The old slot is released first so the new
  booking can reuse it; if the new booking fails for any reason, the original
  is restored to `BOOKED`.
- **Slots are computed, never stored.** Changing a doctor's hours takes
  effect immediately for every future date.
- **A corrupt data row is skipped and logged**, not fatal.

